// WHY: A function parameter `x` shadowed inside an `if` body. The top-level `wait`
// appears BEFORE the `if`, so the inner `let x = 99` does not cross a suspension
// within the if body. However, under Option A (design/concurrency.md §
// ShadowsCrossingLocal), ANY nested `let x` in a suspending function is a compile
// error — the conservative guard rejects all same-name nested shadows regardless of
// position relative to the `wait`.
//
// The root cause: reload_params_from_frame in every continuation state overwrites
// cg.locals["x"] with the frame-slot pointer. If the inner alloca is placed in the
// entry block by Part-A codegen, the reload still runs and restores cg.locals to
// the SLOT pointer — not the inner alloca — corrupting the scope separation. The
// safe answer is a clean compile error until per-binding-ID slot allocation lands
// (roadmap M3c, `v0-3-m3c-shadow-parity`).
//
// This fixture MUST produce a clean compile error (exit 1). No ICE text.
// test-ratchet: R6 flipped this to compile (non-crossing predicate); R7 reverts to
// conservative reject (Option A, blunt param_has_nested_let_shadow guard).
function f(x: int) -> nothing {
  wait sleep(5)
  if (1 > 0) {
    let x = 99
    print(x.toString())
  }
  print(x.toString())
}
function entrypoint() -> nothing {
  f(7)
}
